# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Database files
*.db
*.db-journal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-journal

# Prisma generated AI skills
.agents/
.claude/
.windsurf/
skills-lock.json

# Client source decks dropped into public/ for reference while writing
# case-study copy. Everything under public/ is served at the URL root, so
# a file left here is downloadable by anyone who guesses the name - and
# the deploy pipeline copies the whole tree onto the VPS. These are the
# client's internal materials and are not site assets: nothing in src/
# links to them, they are only cited in comments in work.ts. Keep them on
# disk locally, out of git and off the server.
/public/*.pptx
/public/*.ppt
/public/*.pdf
/public/*.docx

# Same rule, one directory down. The patterns above are anchored to the
# root of public/, so the client's logo artwork - a Cloudflare cue card, a
# MongoDB tent card and a Montra lockup, dropped into public/logos/ - was
# not covered by them and would have shipped.
#
# These are SOURCE artwork, not site assets. The logos actually used by
# the trust bar were extracted from them into public/media/clients (see
# tools/build-client-logos.mjs, which reads from here), and those are
# committed. Nothing in src/ references this directory. Kept on disk so
# the extraction can be re-run or re-tuned without asking the client for
# the files again.
/public/logos/
